[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Your AI agents make decisions all day, and most of them can\'t explain a single one after the fact. Semantica fixes that, an open-source infrastructure layer that turns every inference into a permanent queryable node in a knowledge graph. It just optimized node search on'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a 118,000 node production graph from 24 milliseconds down to 4 microseconds, 6,000 x faster, and it\'s climbing toward 4,000 GitHub stars. Here\'s why graph memory might be the'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'piece your agent stack is missing. Semantica calls itself graph-native infrastructure for context and accountable AI systems. It sits underneath your LLM and your vector store, and instead of just'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'embedding text and hoping similarity search finds the right chunk, it builds a real knowledge graph, entities, relationships, events, and decisions, each with confidence scores and'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provenance attached. The pitch is aimed squarely at teams in finance, health care, legal, and government, places where why did your system decide that is a question you\'re legally required to answer. And the wildest part, it can'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hand a regulator a machine-readable audit file of everything your AI concluded and why. I\'ll show you exactly what that looks like before we\'re done. The project lives under the Semantica AG organization, a young, focused org with'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three public repos and the bio-semantic infrastructure for context, decision systems, and AI. Young doesn\'t mean idle. The main repo has over 2,200 commits. Version 0.61 landed with Databricks Unity Catalog'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'support and named graph RDF backends, and it was pushed to again this morning. The MIT license and the fully self-hostable design are deliberate. This is infrastructure positioning, not a SaaS funnel, and 405 forks suggest'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plenty of teams are already building on it. The problem statement is one most agent builders will recognize. Retrieval augmented generation stores embeddings, numerical fingerprints of text. Embeddings capture similarity, not'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'meaning. They can\'t tell you which facts contradict each other, when a fact stopped being true, or why an agent chose option A over option B last Tuesday. Semantica\'s answer is to make meaning itself the storage format. Extract structured knowledge from every'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'source, detect conflicts before they enter the graph, timestamp every fact twice, when it was true and when you learned it, and record every decision as data you can query later. The repo makes the scope obvious. The processing'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pipeline runs ingest, parse, normalize, split, semantic extraction, conflict detection, and deduplication before anything touches the graph. Storage is polyglot on both sides of the graph'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'world. Neo4j, Falcon DB, Apache Age, and AWS Neptune for property graphs. Or BlazeGraph, Gina, RDF4j, and embedded Oxigraph for RDF triples.'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Vector search plugs into Faiss, Qdrant, Weaviate, Milvus, Pinecone, or plain SQLite. And for the agent crowd, there\'s a full MCP server, so Claude desktop or VS Code can query the graph directly.'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Plus first-class shared context for Agno multi-agent teams. Six stages do the heavy lifting. Ingestion pulls from files, databases, APIs, streams, Databricks, and Snowflake. Extraction'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'runs named entity recognition, relation extraction, and event detection, each with tunable confidence thresholds. Conflict detection compares incoming facts against the graph and flags'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contradictions for review. The knowledge graph merges entities and tracks time. Deterministic reasoning, forward chaining, rete networks, Datalog, SPARQL, derives new facts without asking an LLM to guess.'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the provenance layer records where every single fact came from, down to the source file and the extractor that produced it. Here\'s the feature that separates Semantica from every graph rag library. Decisions are first-class'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'citizens. You record a decision with its category, scenario, reasoning, outcome, and confidence, and it becomes a permanent node. Then the questions that usually die in a Slack thread become'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one-liners. Trace the full causal chain that led to this decision. Find the five most similar past decisions before making a new one. Analyze what downstream work a decision affected. Gate decisions against policy rules'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automatically. Your agent\'s judgment stops being vibes in a context window and becomes an institutional memory. About that 6,000 X, these numbers come from the version 0.5.0 optimization pass, measured on a real 118,000 node'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'production graph. Node search went from 24 milliseconds to 4 microseconds per lookup. That\'s the headline figure, and it\'s the difference between graph queries being a batch job'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and being something you do in line on every agent turn. Semantic deduplication got nearly 7 X faster, and candidate generation dropped by almost 2/3 with a blocking strategy. The methodology is documented in the repo, so you can rerun'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it against your own graph. The fairest way to frame Semantica is against the default stack. Vector-only rag is fantastic at fuzzy recall and trivially easy to set up. Nothing here changes that, and Semantica keeps a vector store'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the loop for similarity search. But vector-only systems have no notion of contradiction, no timeline, and no decision history. The context graph approach costs you a pipeline and an ontology and buys you'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'queryable structure, which facts conflict, what was true in March, which precedent applies, and who told us so. For a chatbot, that\'s overkill. For a loan underwriter or a clinical support'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system, it\'s table stakes. The use cases the project leads with make the line obvious. Anti-money laundering compliance, drug interaction detection, contract analysis with evidence, fraud'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decisions that need justification, incident response timelines. Every one of them is a domain where the answer matters less than the ability to defend it. And here\'s the payoff I promised, the audit file. Semantica\'s provenance'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'layer implements the W3C PROV-O standard, the same vocabulary regulators and data governance teams already understand. Every entity in the graph traces back to a source document, an extractor, and a timestamp. One export'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'call produces a Turtle RDF file you can hand to an auditor, not a slide deck about your AI\'s behavior, but a machine-readable record of it. The readme states the philosophy in one line, getting started is standard'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Python. Pip install Semantica for the core or the all extra for the full suite. The built-in doctor command checks your Python version, build, vector store, and config in one shot. Nice touch for a young project. And'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Semantica Explorer launches a browser-based workbench, a live force-directed view of your graph, a timeline scrubber that replays how facts arrived, causal chain browsing for decisions, and a Shay CL Studio for'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'authoring ontology constraints. 17 command groups cover everything from ingestion to export, so most of the pipeline runs without writing Python at all. The snapshot, nearly 3,900 stars, 455 forks, MIT licensed, pure Python,'

[07:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and shipping fast. The deeper signal is where agent infrastructure is heading. Memory layers were the first wave, accountability layers are next. Because'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the moment agents touch lending, medicine, or government workflows, explainability stops being a feature request and becomes the permit to operate. Semantica is betting that the audit trail belongs in the infrastructure, not'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bolted on afterward. If your agents make decisions that matter, this repo is worth an afternoon. The repo and getsemantica.ai are linked in the description. Subscribe to Prism Labs for weekly deep dives on AI tools and agent'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'infrastructure. See you in the next one.'
